# Files that must never enter this repository: model weights, host sync
# scratch, the Electron runtime, derived caches, and task scratch. Everything
# here is either large, regenerable in one command, or both. The comment on
# each group names the reason, because "why is this ignored" is the only
# question a reader ever asks of this file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model weights. This directory holds 9.0 GB across five GGUF files, mounted
# read only into the engine container at runtime. The repository carries the
# code that drives the weights and the records of what they scored, never the
# weights themselves. Resolved on this machine with:
#   models/gemma-4-E2B-it-Q4_K_M.gguf          3106738272
#   models/mmproj-F16.gguf                      985654080
#   models/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf   2019377600
#   models/Phi-3.5-mini-instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf   2393232672
#   models/qwen2.5-coder-1.5b-instruct-q4_k_m.gguf 1117320768
# The bare patterns below also catch a weight file dropped at the root and the
# two stray empty directories named for weight files that a bad bind mount once
# created: a directory whose name is a weight file name is matched by the file
# patterns, which is why those two never needed a rule of their own.
#
# The directory is anchored to the root on purpose. Written unanchored, `models/`
# also matches a directory of that name anywhere in the tree, which is how the
# record of what these files are stopped being committed: results/models holds
# the header reading, and a pattern meant for the weights swept it up.
/models/
*.gguf
*.ggml
*.safetensors
*.onnx
*.pt
*.pth
*.bin
*.h5
*.ckpt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local research input. The pricing captures and the working paper are inputs to
# this project rather than outputs of it: both are draft, both quote vendors and
# figures that are not settled, and a draft in a repository is a draft that ends
# up quoted somewhere. They were committed once, before this rule existed, so
# they are dropped from the index rather than from disk. The pre-push check
# refuses these two by name as well, because a rule that lives in one file is a
# rule that can be walked past by one command.
/paper/
/pricing-data/

# One paper an outside group wrote, held here to be read rather than to be
# republished, so it leaves the index on the same terms as the two above. It
# carries no named refusal beside it: the pre-push check refuses a closed path
# by name, and a reading list is not a closed path. Nothing has read it, so
# nothing here depends on it.
/research/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local operating procedures. The folder describes how the work is run rather
# than what it found: the procedure that reviews this checkout before any of it
# is published to the public copy lives here, and so does the receipt that
# review writes, which names the commit, the file list and the time it was read.
# Both are the record of this machine's process.
#
# It is held out of both copies on purpose. A published procedure reads as a
# promise about the state of the public copy, and it is not one.
#
# The directory is created by the first review rather than committed, so a
# checkout that has never published does not have one and does not need one.
# `tools/release/checks.py` makes it before writing the receipt. Anchored to the
# root for the same reason the weights are, and the pre-push check refuses this
# path by name as well, because a rule that lives in one file is a rule that can
# be walked past by one command.
/Standard Operation Procedures/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Drive sync scratch. The single entry in .tmp.driveupload is a 2.9 GB
# hardlink to the gemma weights, which means it is one inode with two names:
# committing it would add a copy of a weight file, and deleting it would free
# nothing. Both directions of that are a reason to keep it out.
.tmp.drivedownload/
.tmp.driveupload/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Electron. The installed runtime is 321 MB, including a 100 MB plus framework
# binary and symlinked app bundles that git would store as link data or a
# second copy. The pinned versions live in package-lock.json and one
# `npm install` inside desktop/ restores the whole tree.
desktop/node_modules/
desktop/dist/
desktop/release/
desktop/out/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing material. A Developer ID certificate is a private key with a public
# half: it is what makes a download openable on somebody else's machine, and it
# is also what lets whoever holds it publish an update under this identity. It
# belongs in a keychain and in an environment variable, never in a checkout.
# The extensions are refused as well as the directory, because a key is often
# unpacked somewhere nobody planned for.
#
# `desktop/build/` itself is committed rather than held out: the entitlement
# files and the notarization hook live there, and `lib/DistributionCheck.php`
# refuses the build when the ignore file grows a rule that would drop them.
desktop/build/certs/
*.p12
*.pfx
*.p8
*.cer
*.der
*.mobileprovision
*.provisionprofile

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials and key files. A key belongs in the environment, and a dotenv
# file is where one gets written when that is inconvenient: it was not ignored
# here, which is the gap this block closes. The shape check in
# tools/security/scan_secrets.py refuses a key by what it looks like, and a
# file that is never added cannot depend on a shape somebody forgot to write a
# rule for. The environment names are left unanchored so a dotenv beside a
# nested tool is caught as well; `.env.example` is exempted because a template
# with a placeholder in it is the thing that keeps a real value out.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.envrc
.direnv/
.netrc
*.pem
*.key
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_rsa.*
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.*
credentials.json
service-account*.json

# Compiled artifacts. A static PHP binary is about 20 MB and is produced by
# the build recipe, not committed beside it.
*.phar

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Derived caches. Model fingerprints and cache entries are recomputed from
# the weights and the source on demand, and a stale one is worse than none.
# Anchored to the root for the same reason the weights are: a directory named
# cache somewhere else is somebody's artefact, not this one's.
/cache/

# Task scratch. workspace/ is bind mounted into the harness container as the
# task root and is rewritten by every run.
/workspace/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host engagement state. `plans_and_writeups/track.php` creates `engage/` on the
# first visit and appends an entry on every one after that, so it is the record
# of who read the published pages on whichever host served them rather than a
# part of the viewer. It describes the host and not the project, and every visit
# to the local copy rewrites the working tree, which the release review reads as
# a checkout that holds something besides the commit. The directory is created
# on demand, so a checkout that has never served a page does not have one and
# does not need one.
/plans_and_writeups/engage/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The platform's own copy of a finished run. `kaggle_kernel.py watch --path`
# downloads the whole output tree, which is the same evidence already recorded
# under results/benchmark/kaggle plus the notebook's figures, dataset and zip.
# It is a download, not a measurement: the round trip is recorded by
# kernel-roundtrip.json and the runs by results/benchmark/kaggle, so keeping a
# second copy here would only be a place for the two to disagree.
results/benchmark/kaggle-pull/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Interaction traces. Every run writes a transcript, and so does every probe:
# pressing a row in the window, asking the engine to describe itself, and
# stopping a load test each leave an events.ndjson behind. Those are the run
# narrating itself rather than a measurement, and the load captures are the
# scratch of a test whose result is kept in load-final. engine.log is the
# server's own startup output on this machine, including its accelerator and
# memory lines. All of them describe the device that produced them: absolute
# paths, a loopback address, the temporary directory. The measured documents
# beside them are kept, because those are what a reader compares.
results/agent/ui-*/
results/agent/scriptprobe/
results/agent/stream-probe/
results/agent/fixture-scripted/
results/agent/describe-container/
results/agent/load-harness*/
results/load/
results/engine/engine.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The security reading of this machine's checkout. `security-scan.sh` writes one
# report per run, and every run is a reading of the tree as it stood at that
# minute: the file modes on disk, the hooks installed in this clone, the registry
# as it answered that day. Committing it would be a photograph of a checkout
# rather than a property of the commit, and the next run would disagree with it.
# The reading is taken again by the release review, which refuses a commit on
# what it finds rather than on what an earlier scan recorded.
results/security/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The photographs of the interface. `capture-ui.sh` writes a folder per run,
# named for the minute it ran, and it writes a new one every time by design. A
# picture is the reading no check in this repository takes, which is what makes
# it worth looking at and also what makes it scratch: it is of the layout on the
# day it was drawn, and every later run disagrees with it. The folders are kept
# on the machine that made them rather than in a pile of snapshots.
images/gui/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Heavy media and archives. The four dated photographs are vision fixtures
# read from the host by absolute path; the JSON evidence records the path and
# the model's reading of it, not the bytes. The Kaggle cover images are the
# one exception, since they are small and are submission artifacts.
images/*.jpg
images/*.jpeg
images/*.png
images/*.heic
images/*.tif
images/*.mov
images/*.mp4
!images/jenny-jill-*.jpg
*.zip
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.7z
*.dmg
*.iso

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# macOS. A .DS_Store is written into every directory the Finder touches, and
# this tree has picked up five of them.
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
.DocumentRevisions-V100
.fseventsd
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python environments. A virtual environment is 1.1 GB of other people's
# installed packages, rebuilt from a manifest in one command, and it describes
# the machine that made it rather than this project. The one here also happens
# to carry a `.gitignore` holding `*`, which keeps its contents out of a status
# listing as an accident of how it was created rather than as a rule this
# repository states; the rule is stated here so it holds for any environment.
# The pre-push check refuses the same directory, because a rule that lives in
# one file is a rule that can be walked past by one command.
/venv_q_mdl_tester/
/.venv/
/venv/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python bytecode. tools/kaggle/ is the portable benchmark and the kernel
# builder, and importing either leaves a __pycache__ per package. The .pyc is
# a build product of the .py beside it, and a committed one is a second copy of
# the source that can disagree with it.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The quick tester's own machine state. `models.toml` and `modes.toml` are the
# registry and travel with the repository; `settings.local.json` says which
# platform this machine builds for, where its weights are and where its
# transcripts go, which describes the machine rather than the project. The
# environment the installer creates is the same kind of thing as any other
# virtual environment and is refused here as well, by full path so that a folder
# of the same name further down is not caught by accident.
local-model-quick-tester/settings.local.json
/.venv-local-model-quick-tester/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model test transcripts. `local-model-quick-tester/` writes a markdown copy of
# every conversation into the directory the `transcript_dir` setting names, and
# that setting points at `model-tests/` beside this file by default. A
# conversation is a scratch pad rather than a measurement: it carries the
# prompts that were tried, the model's raw output for each, and the notes of
# whoever was at the keyboard. It is also where a scratch script against a
# hosted model gets saved, which is a second reason for the directory to stay
# on the machine that made it.
#
# The directory is created on the first write and is not committed, so a
# checkout that has never run the tester does not have one and does not need
# one. `quicktester/transcript.py` calls `mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)`
# before it writes, and so does the screen that opens the folder, so it appears
# on demand rather than being asked of a reader by hand. Anchored to the root
# for the same reason the weights are: a directory of this name further down is
# somebody else's artefact.
#
# The rule is not the only defence. The pre-push check refuses this path by
# name as well, because a rule that lives in one file is a rule that can be
# walked past by one command.
/model-tests/
